title: Save buffer
pid: f413caa8-6d86-489b-94d4-db191502f2ef
body: |2-
    - Request: First =((request . "save buffer") (view-id . "VIEW_ID") (point-lines-below-focused-headline . "N") (point-column . "C") (point-screen-lines-below-window-start . "M"))\n=, then one UTF-8 length-prefixed S-expression envelope:
      #+begin_src lisp
      ((saved-buffer "...Org text...")
       (baseline "...the saved buffer's text as the server last drew it...")
       (other-views
         (((view-id "...") (dirty false))
          ((view-id "...") (dirty true)
           (baseline (present "...last clean text..."))
           (current "...edited text..."))
          ((view-id "...") (dirty true)
           (baseline unavailable)
           (current "...edited text...")))))
      #+end_src
      =Content-Length= is the envelope's UTF-8 byte length. =baseline= is
      mandatory: the saved buffer's baseline, the text the server last
      drew for it, as the client installed it (without the focus and
      fold metadata the client removes) and before any edit (Emacs keeps it in
      'skg-clean-baseline', Neovim in 'vim.b.skg_clean_baseline',
      skg-for-ai in the view's '.full.org' file). A new empty view's
      baseline is the text the client created it with (see "The
      baseline of a view" below). It has no =unavailable= form. A save whose envelope lacks it is refused,
      with an error naming the field, and nothing is written. =other-views=
      is mandatory even when empty and covers every other live client view,
      including views the server has not registered. Clean entries carry no
      buffer snapshots. Dirty entries carry exact current text and either the exact
      clean baseline or explicit =unavailable=. Duplicate view IDs, the saved view ID,
      missing fields, and inconsistent clean/dirty fields are rejected. Raw Org
      in place of the envelope is not a supported legacy form.
      - Two optional fields drive the fork-confirmation round-trip
        (below): =(approved-forks . "true")= marks a re-issued save whose
        forks the user approved, and
        =(fork-repos ((N . REPO) ...))= pairs each forked node's id
        N with the owned skgrepo chosen for its clone. Both are absent on
        an ordinary save.
      - =(strandeds-reviewed . "true")= and =(reset-strandeds ("RELATION"
        "RECORDER" "MEMBER") ...)= are present only on a retry after
        =strandeds-confirmation=: the first says the user reviewed the
        stranded relationships, the second names those to reset to default
        (possibly none).
      - =(delete-from . "visible")= or =(delete-from . "all")= is
        present only on a retry after =delete-from-confirmation=: the
        user chose to strip the deleted IDs from the visible records
        only, or from every owned record. It rides that save and its
        retries, and is never cached.
      - =(approved-hoist-pids "PID" ...)= is present only on a retry
        after =telescope-hoist-confirmation=. It is the exact list the
        user approved for text publication; it is not a boolean and
        does not authorize any other PID or bypass ordinary save
        validation.
      - A save carries no =(approved-overPrivateText-pids ...)=: the
        text-release approval of a save's rerender rides the "Rerender
        views" request that follows the save's
        =overPrivateText-telescope-confirmation= (below), not the save.
        It is independent of Hoist authority: viewing text and publishing
        it on disk are different decisions.
      - =(diff-view-buffer . "NAME")= is optional: it names the buffer
        of the diff view the saved buffer is, by the =buffer= name of the
        "Diff view" request (="all changes"=, ="merges"=, ="skgrepo
        changes"=, ="order-only changes"=). It matters only for a view
        the connection does not hold (a connection that ends with each
        command, as skg-for-ai's does without its daemon, or one opened
        after a server restart): the save then re-renders the buffer as that buffer of
        the diff view, with its processed summary, keeps every viewnode
        ID it carries (see =(vnid X)= under "The metadata headers"), and
        leaves the connection holding it as that buffer, so that an
        "Extend diff view" request can follow. A view the
        connection holds keeps the kind it was registered with. A name
        no buffer has refuses the save, and nothing is written. Both
        clients keep the name in each buffer of the diff view they open
        and send it on its saves, as skg-for-ai does.
      - =(perspective . "skgrepo SKGREPO")= is optional:
        it names the perspective the saved buffer was drawn from,
        when that is not its root homes (see
        "Set perspective"). The save checks the buffer's
        =(inPlaceOf N)= parts against, and rerenders it from,
        the perspective it names, and the connection keeps the perspective
        for the view, for the rerenders it starts itself. Without it, or
        with =(perspective . "root homes")=, the buffer is drawn from
        its root homes. A held view's earlier perspective yields to
        the one its save names, which is how a buffer the server lost in
        a restart regains it. Any other value, a skgrepo the config
        does not list after "skgrepo" included, refuses the save, and
        nothing is written. A save need not name a perspective in the
        view's cycle. Each client keeps the perspective in the buffer
        and sends it only while it is not the root homes.
      - =(preview . "true")= is optional: it asks only what the save
        would hide and stop hiding, and writes nothing. The server builds
        the save's plan and checks it as for a save, up to the
        stale-buffer check and the restore and processed refusals below,
        then answers, after =save-lock= and in place of every other
        terminal message, =((response-type save-preview) (hides ("..."
        ...)) (errors (...)) (warnings (...)))=: the =hides= lines a
        save-result would carry (below), present even when empty, and
        the errors that would refuse the save, worded as a
        save-result's, when it would be refused. A request the server
        refuses before reading its plan (a bad envelope, a bad
        =perspective=) is answered =save-preview= too. It asks no
        question, so a preview of a save that would fork, Hoist, ask
        about strandeds or about deletes says only what it would hide;
        it writes nothing, registers no view, rerenders none, and
        consumes no processed request. An older server ignores the
        field and saves, so a client sends it only to a server whose
        "Verify connection" answer names ="save preview"=. skg-for-ai's
        'save' without '--yes' sends one; neither editor client does.
      - =point-lines-below-focused-headline= is global to the buffer save: the number of text lines from the focused headline to point before save.
      - =point-column= is global to the buffer save: the column of point within its line, echoed back so the client can restore the exact cursor position.
      - =point-screen-lines-below-window-start= is global to the buffer save: the number of screen lines from the window's top line to point before save. The server echoes all three point fields in the final save response so Emacs can restore point and scroll position after replacing the buffer text.
    - Response: Multiple length-prefixed messages, sent sequentially:
      1. Early lock acknowledgement (sent immediately, before reading the envelope):
         =Content-Length: N\r\n\r\n((response-type save-lock) (lock-views ("view ID1" "view ID2" ...)))=
         Both clients lock every local Skg view before taking the request's
         buffer snapshots. This message acknowledges that broad lock; its server-known
         view ID list does not authorize unlocking client-only views.
      2. Save-relax-lock message (sent after preparation and conflict checking, before mutation):
         =Content-Length: N\r\n\r\n((response-type save-relax-lock) (lock-views ("view ID1" "view ID2" ...)))=
         The keep-set contains selected collateral targets and every dirty view
         whose baseline/current buffer snapshots were checked, including unregistered
         views. The saved view is retained implicitly. Only unrelated clean
         views may unlock at this point. A malformed save-relax-lock message leaves
         all locks held until terminal cleanup.
      3. Zero or more collateral-view messages (one per collateral view, streamed as each finishes):
         =Content-Length: N\r\n\r\n((response-type collateral-view) (view-id "VIEW_ID") (content "..."))=
         No dirty view is among them. Each client unlocks and updates
         each buffer as it arrives, and keeps its text as the buffer's
         baseline; a client never replaces a buffer with unsaved edits,
         but leaves it as it is and names it in a message. An update
         keeps point, and the fold of every headline the buffer
         already showed, by viewnode ID. The content's =folded= and
         =bodyFolded= atoms are those of the view's last save, so the
         client decides them anew: a headline is =folded= when the
         parent it now has hid its children before the update, and
         =bodyFolded= when it hid only its own body, or hid its
         children and has none now. The content's atoms stand where
         the deciding headline is new in it.
      4. Final save response:
         =Content-Length: N\r\n\r\n((response-type save-result) (content "...") (errors ("..." ...)) (warnings ("..." ...)) (point-lines-below-focused-headline N) (point-column C) (point-screen-lines-below-window-start M))=
         =content= is the re-rendered saved buffer (nil on failure).
         The client keeps the content as the buffer's new baseline; a
         failed save carries none, and the buffer keeps its own. =errors= is a list of failure-explaining strings. =warnings= is a list of nonfatal messages. Both lists are present and empty if none.
         The refresh of each view the save rerenders says nothing of
         what it changed there ('docs/glossary.org', "what the refresh
         changes silently").
         Each message names the headlines it concerns as subheadlines
         (see "How a message names a headline" below). A failed save
         sends one error, =NOTHING WAS SAVED=, whose validation errors
         follow as =** Error N= headlines.
         When the save hid something, stopped hiding something, or took
         a child out of a forker without hiding it, the answer
         carries =(hides ("LINE" ...))=, after the point entries and the
         processed summary (below) and before =changed-headlines=, one
         line each, in words the server chooses
         ('hide_report_lines' in 'server/from_text/hide_report.rs'):
         - =Hides 'M' from 'S', as content of 'E'.= for each member M
           the save adds to note S's =hides=, naming each member
           E of S's forks folder (a note S forks, or a forker or an
           indirect member S does not hide) whose content holds M (the clause is absent
           for a hide outside every such partner);
         - =Stops hiding 'M' from 'S', as content of 'E'.= for each it
           removes;
         - =Hides nothing: 'R' left 'S', but WHY.= for each child R that
           left the content of an owned note S, which has such a fork
           partner, whose departure hid nothing. WHY names the rule:
           =it is no content of 'E', nor a mirror of any= (R neither is a
           member of a partner's content nor a mirror of one, as a
           note's own fork of it is), =it was hidden already= (or
           ='M', which it stands for, was hidden already=), ='S' still
           contains 'M', which it stands for=, =this save shows it
           under 'E'= (an explicit gesture in the forks folder won), or
           =this save places it under 'E'= (R moved under a member as
           such E, whose content it joins), or ='Y' keeps it= (or ='Y'
           keeps 'M', which it stands for=; several keepers =keep=):
           an owned note Y other than S contains it after the save, so
           no member of the forks folder shows it ('docs/forks.org', "What you keep
           elsewhere").
         Each quoted name is a title, as the save leaves it. A fork's
         clone counts as a forker with no hides on disk, named =the
         fork 'T'=, since it has its original's title; a node the save
         deletes is named in no line. Only records the skgrepo
         restriction shows count. The hides are computed from the save's
         plan against the graph, so they are what the plan writes,
         whatever gesture asked for them (a removal, a forks-folder
         edit, a hidden folder's edit, a forking edit); what merges,
         restore requests and delete propagation add after the plan is
         not reported. When the server withholds the save's rerender
         (=overPrivateText-telescope-confirmation=, below), the save is
         written but its hides are not sent. Emacs shows the
         lines in the echo area, below the message the save already
         showed; Neovim notifies them as one message; skg-for-ai prints
         them after the server's counts.
         When the saved buffer is one of the diff view's, the point
         entries are followed by =(processed-summary (processed N) (changed M) (foreign F) (order-only O) (waiting W) (notEditable K))=:
         of the M changed nodes of the owned skgrepos (O of them changed only
         in the order of some list), N are processed, and W of those N are
         held back by a commit of the processed, which would wait for a
         node that does not go in (=docs/diff-view.org=, "Which nodes go
         in"); F nodes changed in foreign skgrepos, counted by the stems
         of their changed =.skg= files; and K changes are not editable in the view: they
         are recorded by nodes whose changes the buffer does not make editable. A
         buffer makes a node's changes editable where it has an editable
         occurrence of it, in content or in a partner folder, or a
         ghost of it in content position: under the buffer
         root or an occurrence, or in the old content of such a
         ghost. The buffer is refreshed over the graph straddle: its
         roots, and every headline it holds editable, stay as saved, and
         its change facts, change viewnodes and removed members are
         recomputed. What it adds anew it adds by its pruned
         change view sets recomputed after the save: a new member in U is editable,
         with its content list, and any other new member is a
         write-protected member shown without its content. It
         makes no occurrence editable and appends no root; the "extend
         diff view" request below does, and so does the rerender that
         follows a "Repo files reloaded" message. An editable-view request
         cascades as in any view. Every extension spends
         =initial_node_limit=, but the budget refuses only the extension
         of a node outside U; nothing else in the buffer is bounded by
         it.
         After everything else comes =(changed-headlines (V ...))=, the
         redraw delta from the saved buffer's text (the envelope's
         =saved-buffer=) to =content=, when the two differ only in
         headline lines (see "The redraw delta" below). Each V is a
         viewnode ID, a bare symbol; =(changed-headlines ())= means no
         headline changed. Without the entry, the client redraws the
         whole buffer. A failed save and every confirmation carry none.
    - Processed requests (=(processed OBJECTS)= and =(unprocessed OBJECTS)=
      inside a headline's =(editRequest ...)=; see "Metadata of the
      diff view") are
      consumed by a save of any buffer, not only one of the diff view's,
      after its graph edits succeed: each sets or clears the marks of the
      change-forest leaves its headline names, at their current
      fingerprints. A save that stops for a confirmation (delete-from,
      strandeds, Hoist, forks) or fails consumes none, and leaves them in
      the buffer. Under a skgrepo restriction a save carrying a processed
      request, or a restore request, is refused before anything is
      written: =A processed request requires no skgrepo restriction= (or
      =A restore request ...=), in the words of the diff view's refusal. A request that names no current change is dropped, with
      the warning =A processed request names no current change; it was
      dropped.=, which names its headline as a subheadline.
    - A =(folder containers)= request on an occurrence that the refresh
      leaves with no =containerFolder=, since nothing but its
      view-ancestors contains it, if anything does, adds the warning
      =No other containers.=, which names each such headline as a
      subheadline ('no_other_containers_warning' in
      'server/rerender_pass.rs').
    - A save from a stale buffer is refused only if the buffer edited one
      of its stale graphnodes, before anything is written and before any
      confirmation is asked ('stale_write_errors' in
      'server/serve/handlers/save_staleness.rs'). A save writes a
      graphnode either from the buffer's copy of it, the Save
      instruction its saved text yields, or from the graph as it stands
      at save time; only the buffer's copy can be older than the graph,
      and only a write from it, or a delete, can discard graph state the
      user never saw. What the buffer showed before any edit is its
      baseline (see "The baseline of a view" below). So the server
      parses the baseline as it parses the saved text, up to the filter
      that drops nodeInstructions identical to the graph as no-ops, under
      the same skgrepo restriction; what survives are the baseline's
      writes, the graphnodes whose copy in the baseline no longer
      matches the graph (or that the graph no longer has): the stale
      ones. A stale graphnode the buffer shows exactly as its
      baseline does (compared as a view reads a graphnode back: its
      relationship lists by distinct pid, its body without trailing
      whitespace) is unedited: the user did not edit it, and writing
      it would only undo what changed. The save leaves it out, before
      the plan's validators judge it ('without_unedited_stale_saves'
      in the same file): its write, its move back to the home skgrepo
      the buffer shows, and, if it is foreign, its fork. The rest of the
      save proceeds, and its response carries one warning per graphnode
      left out, =Left as the graph has it: TITLE (ID), which changed
      after this buffer was rendered and which this buffer did not
      edit.=, TITLE as the buffer shows it, or =... which was deleted
      after ...= for one the graph no longer has; the redraw shows the
      graph's version. A write-protected occurrence, which writes
      nothing, is not refused for showing a title or home skgrepo that
      an occurrence of its graphnode in the baseline shows, editable or
      write-protected, though the graph changed it since: it is no edit.
      A stale graphnode the buffer edits or deletes is an edited stale
      graphnode: writing the buffer's text of it would silently undo a
      change made since the buffer was drawn. A delete is never
      unedited. What the buffer writes is what its text yields after
      that filter, minus its unedited stale graphnodes, plus the original of
      each fork clone; the writes the save derives from the current
      graph (nodeMerges, delete propagation, strandeds resets, Hoist
      repairs, restore requests) undo nothing and are not checked. So a
      node the buffer shows as the graph has it, or as its baseline
      does, never refuses a save, however old the buffer; a node the
      graph deleted after the buffer was drawn is left out if the
      buffer did not edit it and refuses the save if it did, so an old buffer
      cannot recreate it; a merge request is never stale; and a
      restart, a rebuild or a body-whitespace strip makes nothing stale
      by itself. A graphnode the buffer writes
      without holding a copy of it in its baseline (pasted in by ID, or
      typed into a new empty view) has nothing to compare, and is not
      checked. A baseline that cannot be parsed refuses the save, saying
      so. Each edited stale graphnode is one =** Error N= of the =NOTHING WAS SAVED=
      message, whose first line reads "A node this save would write
      changed after the buffer was rendered:", naming the buffer's
      occurrences of it as subheadlines, and saying what changed: the
      graph's title of it, its ID, and the aspects that differ from the
      baseline's copy ("the title (then "X", now "Y")", "the body", "the
      content (gained IDS, lost IDS)", "the home skgrepo (then A, now
      B)", and by name the aliases, extra IDs, flags, forks and
      hides); a changed body is followed by its changed
      lines, baseline against graph, each lost line prefixed "- " and
      each gained one "+ ", at most 40 each way. A deleted node's error
      says instead "It was deleted after this buffer was rendered." The
      user's own edits are the diff of the buffer against the same
      baseline, which the recovery command writes. There is no
      save-anyway path: the user reopens a fresh view and reapplies the
      edit; the refused buffer keeps its text. Since each connection
      rerenders only its own views, this is what protects a buffer from
      another connection's save (another client, or 'tools/skg-for-ai'),
      and from files reloaded after another writer changed them.
    - A save of a view the connection holds is refused, before the
      stale-buffer check and before anything is written, when the view
      was last drawn under a skgrepo restriction showing another set of
      skgrepos than the connection's current one
      ('error_unless_view_was_rendered_under_current_restriction' in
      'server/serve/handlers/save_buffer.rs'). The server records, for
      each view it holds, the restriction it was last drawn under: at
      the view request, at each rerender, and at the save's own redraw.
      A buffer drawn under another restriction says nothing of what
      that restriction kept out, or shows what the current one keeps
      out, so its text means something else under the current one. The
      refusal reads "This view was rendered under a different skgrepo
      restriction (then: main, private; now: main). Rerender it, or
      archive its edits and reopen it." A restriction switch rerenders
      every open view, and the clients refuse to switch while a view has
      unsaved edits; this makes the server independent of that courtesy,
      and covers any change of the connection's restriction that
      rerenders no view. A rebuild is one ("Rebuild session-lifetime
      stores"): when the reloaded config changes the set of skgrepos
      the connection shows, a view with unsaved edits, which the rerender
      after the rebuild skips, is refused at its save by this rule. A
      buffer the connection does not hold has no record and is not
      checked.
    - A write-protected occurrence saves nothing of its own, so a save is
      refused, before anything is written, if one differs from the graph
      ('errors_for_writeProtected_occurrences' in
      'server/from_text/write_protected_edits.rs'): in its title or home
      skgrepo (unless the buffer's baseline shows the same, the graph
      having changed since: see the stale buffer above), or in its alias
      folder, when present, by lacking or
      reordering one of the node's aliases or by carrying a relRepo
      request. Under a write-protected occurrence no child claims
      membership, so anything else standing there -- an alias the node
      lacks, a child, a folder's members -- is the user's note or the
      diff view's, which the refresh shows unwarranted, and is not
      checked. An ID folder, under any node, is refused only when it
      lacks one of the node's IDs; and a present flags folder, under any
      node, must equal the node's flags.
    - Before mutation, the server computes the update-relevant neighborhood of
      every prepared write/delete identity independently in the before and
      candidate graphs, twice: over every ordinary relation, and over them
      without links ('SaveAffectedIds' in
      'server/serve/handlers/save_dependencies/mod.rs'). Other registered views
      containing an unrestricted ID of the full neighborhood are rerendered,
      except any the save envelope's other-views marks dirty. For
      every dirty client view, the server parses and unions
      dependencies from its clean baseline, current text, and registered viewforest
      when present. A dependency is a graphnode the view holds a viewnode of: an
      occurrence, the original an occurrence's =(inPlaceOf N)= names, a gnid-only viewnode, a
      ghost, or an ID property member. A merge request's target and a link's
      target are not dependencies merely by being named there: the request or
      the link holds no copy of the target, so a save that changes the target
      clobbers nothing the view holds.
      Any intersection with the neighborhood without links refuses the save
      conservatively. The dirty-view match ignores links in both directions: a
      dirty view of L, where L links to a saved A, does not conflict, nor does a
      dirty view showing A conflict with a save that only adds a link to A. The
      collateral redraw does not ignore them, but it skips every dirty view: a
      dirty view is never redrawn, even one that reaches the save only through a
      link, so what a link changes in it (A's mentionerFolder, say) is refreshed
      at its next redraw. Malformed
      or missing dirty dependency data also refuses. A refusal returns
      =save-result= with nil content and actionable errors naming view IDs and
      IDs, and performs no filesystem, graph, search index, registry, or collateral
      update. There is no save-anyway path.
    - Deleting under a skgrepo restriction: a delete's propagation strips
      the deleted IDs from every owned recorder ('apply_delete_propagation
      _cleanup' in 'server/save.rs'). When records in sections the
      skgrepo restriction keeps out name a deleted graphnode and the request
      lacks =(delete-from ...)=, nothing is skgsave-committed and the
      server replies, before any other decision and in place of
      save-result, =((response-type delete-from-confirmation) (count N)
      (prompt "..."))=, N the number of such records, with no IDs. The
      clients ask "Delete from: (c)ancel, (v)isible records only, (a)ll
      owned records" and re-issue the same save with
      =(delete-from . "visible")= or =(delete-from . "all")=; cancel
      saves nothing. Under "visible" the restricted records stay as they
      are, and the dangling ID shows as an unknown viewnode where its
      recorder is shown; under "all" every owned record of the deleted
      IDs goes, as with no skgrepo restriction.
    - A nodeMerge is built from the graph as the save's ordinary edits
      leave it: the server prepares the buffer's other writes first, then
      builds each merge request's three writes (the text preserver, the
      updated acquirer, the deleted acquiree) from that candidate graph
      ('prepare_save_under_mutation_gate' in 'server/save.rs'). So an
      edit the same save makes to the acquirer or to the acquiree is
      kept, and the merged contents lack what the save deletes. A save
      whose buffer deletes the acquirer or the acquiree of one of its own
      merge requests is refused before anything is written, with an
      error that begins =A node this save would merge is also deleted
      by it= and gives the node's title and ID and which of the two it
      is; only the buffer's own deletes count, not delete propagation.
    - Moving a node between skgrepos: for each home skgrepo move the server
      finds every relationship touching the moved node, in either
      direction and whether or not a view shows it, that sat at its
      default relRepo and that the sticky-else-default rule would leave
      off its new default ('server/from_text/strandeds.rs'). One left more
      public than its member's new home (an inbound relationship of a
      privatizing move) is raised to the new default, and the save's
      warnings say so. If any other remains and the request lacks
      =(strandeds-reviewed . "true")=, nothing is skgsave-committed and the
      server replies, before any other decision but the delete-from
      confirmation and in place of save-result,
      =((response-type strandeds-confirmation) (strandeds (((relation
      "contains") (recorder "R") (member "M") (relRepo "REPO") (default
      "REPO")) ...)) (prompt "..."))=, with IDs but no titles. The
      client re-issues the same save with =(strandeds-reviewed . "true")=
      and the chosen =(reset-strandeds ...)=; the server resets those
      to their new defaults, saving each recorder (outside the view or
      not), so the views that show them rerender as collateral.
    - The first alternative terminal message: after validation, a save
      rereads every telescope it will write. If any currently selects a
      title or body below home and lacks exact approval, nothing is
      skgsave-committed and the server replies, in place of save-result,
      =((response-type telescope-hoist-confirmation) (telescopes
      (((pid "P") (home "REPO")) ...)) (prompt "..."))=.
      This response contains no title or body. The prompt explains that
      Hoist moves the selected title/body text to home and removes lower
      title/body copies while retaining lower relationships and aliases;
      Abort writes nothing and requires manual =.skg= repair. Hoist
      re-issues the same save with =(approved-hoist-pids "P" ...)=.
      The server rereads and reclassifies on that retry, so newly dirty
      PIDs produce another batch confirmation. Each attempt still sends
      =save-lock= first, and the confirmation is terminal for that
      attempt: neither =save-relax-lock=, =collateral-view=, nor
      =save-result= follows it.
    - The second alternative terminal message: a save that detects forks
      (edited foreign nodes, or explicit fork requests) and does not
      carry =(approved-forks . "true")= skgsave-commits nothing and replies, in
      place of save-result,
      =((response-type fork-confirmation) (content "...") (to-minibuffer "..."))=,
      where =content= is the fork-confirmation buffer (an instructions
      headline, then per fork the clone-to-be over the original it
      forks; an unspecified clone skgrepo renders as the PICK-A-REPO
      placeholder under a =# Suggested repo ...= comment). The client
      shows it, collects a skgrepo per placeholder, and on approval
      re-issues the same save with =(approved-forks . "true")= and
      =(fork-repos ...)=. Hoist is decided first: when a save needs
      both decisions, its approved Hoist retry then returns the fork
      confirmation, and neither attempt writes.
    - The third alternative terminal message protects the saved and
      collateral rerenders. After a valid save has updated disk and the
      session-lifetime stores, the server renders the saved view and every collateral view in memory.
      Under a restricted skgrepo-set, if the staged forests contain overPrivateText
      telescopes, it streams no content, and sends
      =((response-type overPrivateText-telescope-confirmation)
      (operation save-rerender) (pids ("P" ...)) (prompt "...")
      (view-ids ("SAVED_VIEW_ID" "COLLATERAL_VIEW_ID" ...)))=:
      the views whose rerender is withheld, the saved view first.
      The save itself has succeeded; only its updated text is withheld.
      The server registers the saved view as the save left it (with the
      IDs the save gave its new nodes), sending none of it; the
      collateral views keep their registrations. To approve, the client
      sends "Rerender views" (below) naming those views, with
      =(approved-overPrivateText-pids "P" ...)=: it answers with their
      rerenders, and writes nothing. The save is never sent again for
      this, because it would apply a second time: its edits of existing
      graphnodes now match the graph and are not written again, a new
      graphnode it put under a container would make that container an
      edited stale graphnode (the stale-buffer rule above) and so refuse it, and a new root of
      the view would be created a second time. On an unrestricted connection the
      ordinary save-result carries the shared overPrivateText-telescope
      warning. Declining leaves the client's buffers as they are.
    - Exactly one of =save-result=, =save-preview=, =delete-from-confirmation=,
      =strandeds-confirmation=,
      =telescope-hoist-confirmation=, =fork-confirmation=, and the save-rerender
      =overPrivateText-telescope-confirmation= is sent as the terminal response to
      one save attempt.
    - If the server errors before sending the early lock message (e.g. malformed request), only one message is sent: the error response in the save-result format.
